import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.dWYrTn1b.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Output contract","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/output.md","filePath":"reference/output.md"}'),n={name:"reference/output.md"};function d(r,e,i,c,h,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="output-contract" tabindex="-1">Output contract <a class="header-anchor" href="#output-contract" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Output contract&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A target turns the resolved program and its facts into the files a robot stack runs. This page fixes what every target&#39;s output guarantees, and then the files of the first target, <code>ros-gz-nav2</code>.</p><h2 id="rules-for-every-target" tabindex="-1">Rules for every target <a class="header-anchor" href="#rules-for-every-target" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rules for every target&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>One directory, runnable by path.</strong> <code>--output DIR</code> receives an ordinary directory. The user builds nothing and installs nothing to run it; the entry point is a launch file inside it. Files of the same name are overwritten; other files in the directory are left alone. The compiler refuses an output directory that is, or contains, a source file or a library.</p><p><strong>One authoritative file per fact.</strong> A fact appears in the one file whose consumer needs it, and every other file that needs the same fact is derived from the same analysis value at compile time, never re-derived at run time. The table in each target&#39;s section says which file owns which fact.</p><p><strong>No absolute paths.</strong> A file on disk never contains the output directory&#39;s path, so the directory can be moved or mounted elsewhere. Where a consumer demands an absolute path at run time (a simulator plugin that opens a parameter file by name), the file holds the token <code>\${OUTPUT_DIR}</code> and the launch file substitutes the real path when it starts the process.</p><p><strong>Deterministic.</strong> The same program, libraries and compiler version produce byte-identical output: no timestamps, no host names, no random ids.</p><p><strong>Execution input and inspection output are separate.</strong> The runtime reads only the files under <code>robot/</code>, <code>world/</code>, <code>nav/</code>, <code>task/</code>, <code>launch/</code> and <code>runtime/</code>. <code>compile.json</code> is for people and tools: the facts, the validation results, the approximations the target made, and the sources and parts the output came from. Deleting it changes nothing at run time.</p><p><strong>Approximations are declared.</strong> Where the target stands in for something it cannot simulate as such (a mecanum wheel as a sphere with anisotropic friction, a lidar with a default sample count), it says so in <code>compile.json</code> under <code>approximations</code>, with what was approximated, how, and why. Nothing is approximated silently, and nothing the target cannot do is emitted as if it could: an unsupported construct is a <code>target.*</code> diagnostic that stops emission.</p><p><strong>The task stays goal-agnostic in the tree and goal-bound in the mission.</strong> The behavior tree names what to do; the mission file holds the poses the tree&#39;s goals resolve to. A target&#39;s own executor reads both.</p><h2 id="ros-gz-nav2" tabindex="-1"><code>ros-gz-nav2</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#ros-gz-nav2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;\`ros-gz-nav2\`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>ROS 2 Jazzy, Gazebo Harmonic, <code>ros2_control</code>, and Nav2. The output is:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>&lt;output&gt;/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── robot/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── robot.urdf            links, joints, inertials, visuals, collisions,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   │                         ros2_control interfaces, Gazebo sensor and friction blocks</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── controllers.yaml      controller_manager, the drive controller, joint states</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   └── meshes/&lt;part-id&gt;/     the visual meshes, copied from the libraries</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── world/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── world.sdf             physics, lighting, ground plane, scene entities</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   └── bridge.yaml           ros_gz_bridge topics: clock, sensors</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── nav/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── nav2.base.yaml        plugin choices and constants</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── nav2.robot.yaml       what the robot decides: footprint, inflation, kinematics, AMCL start</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── map.pgm               the occupancy grid drawn from the world&#39;s obstacles</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   └── map.yaml              its resolution and origin, for the map server</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── task/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── behavior_tree.xml     the task as a BehaviorTree.CPP tree over the vocabulary&#39;s actions</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   ├── mission.json          the goal poses and the time budget</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   └── evaluation.json       the checks, and the planar shapes of the parts and entities</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── launch/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   └── bringup.launch.py     the entry point: simulator, robot, controllers, navigation, task</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├── runtime/                  the task executor and its adapters, copied here; needs only ROS 2</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>└── compile.json              inspection: facts, validation, approximations, provenance</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Nav2 reads parameter files in order with later files winning, so the launch file passes <code>nav2.base.yaml</code> then <code>nav2.robot.yaml</code>. Everything the robot decides is in the second file and nowhere else.</p><p><strong>Status.</strong> Every file above is emitted. The launch brings up the simulator, the robot, the drive controller, Nav2 and the task runtime, in that order, and shuts everything down when the task ends (<code>exit_on_task_end:=false</code> keeps it running; <code>navigation:=false</code> brings up only the simulator, the robot and its drive controller, to drive it by hand on <code>/cmd_vel</code>). The task runtime writes <code>run/task_result.json</code> under the output directory (<code>run_dir:=DIR</code> moves it); <code>run/</code> is never written by the compiler, so a rerun of the compiler leaves it alone. Navigation has no map and no localization yet: the world frame <code>map</code> meets <code>odom</code> at the spawn pose through a static transform, and both costmaps are filled by the laser alone.</p><h3 id="frames-and-topics" tabindex="-1">Frames and topics <a class="header-anchor" href="#frames-and-topics" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Frames and topics&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>base_footprint</code> is the root link: the ground projection of the robot, the frame the spawn pose places, and the controller&#39;s base frame. <code>base_link</code> is the chassis part frame; every other link is named after its component and its joint <code>&lt;component&gt;_joint</code>.</li><li>The base takes <code>geometry_msgs/msg/TwistStamped</code> on <code>/cmd_vel</code>, whatever the controller; it publishes odometry on <code>/odom</code> and <code>odom -&gt; base_footprint</code> on <code>/tf</code>. A stale command (older than 0.5 s) stops the base. Mecanum, two-wheel differential, skid-steer, tricycle, Ackermann and omni bases are bound to the matching <code>ros2_control</code> controllers; a steered wheel hangs from a kingpin joint named <code>&lt;component&gt;_steer_joint</code>, and wheels that only roll are observed but not commanded. An omni base is bound to <code>velocity_controllers/JointGroupVelocityController</code>, and <code>runtime/run_omni_drive.py</code> turns <code>/cmd_vel</code> into the wheel speeds from each wheel&#39;s position and rolling direction (<code>robot/omni_drive.json</code>) and publishes the odometry from the measured wheel speeds; ros2_controllers&#39; omni controller models only wheels evenly spaced on a circle, and the catalog&#39;s four-wheel omni bases sit on rectangles.</li><li>A base that steers plans with Smac Hybrid-A* at its minimum turning radius (wheelbase over the tangent of the steering limit) and follows with MPPI&#39;s Ackermann model; it is not held to a final heading, since it cannot turn in place to fix one.</li><li>Sensors publish under their role name: the first laser on <code>/scan</code>, the first spinning lidar on <code>/lidar3d/scan</code> (its middle ring) and <code>/lidar3d/points</code>, the first IMU on <code>/imu</code>, the first camera on <code>/camera/image_raw</code> and <code>/camera/camera_info</code>, the first depth camera on <code>/depth/image_raw</code> and <code>/depth/points</code>, the first RGBD camera on <code>/rgbd/image_raw</code>, <code>/rgbd/depth/image_raw</code>, <code>/rgbd/points</code> and <code>/rgbd/camera_info</code>, the first ultrasonic range finder on <code>/ultrasonic/range</code> (its cone of beams on <code>/ultrasonic/points</code>), the first bumper on <code>/bumper</code> (<code>ros_gz_interfaces/msg/Contacts</code>); a second sensor of a kind is suffixed with its component name. Frames are the component names.</li><li>The costmaps mark what every point cloud sees, points 0.05 to 2 m above the ground, and with the localizer read one scan. It is the first laser&#39;s; without a laser it is the first spinning lidar&#39;s middle ring; a robot with neither but with a depth or RGBD camera gets <code>/scan</code> from <code>runtime/run_depth_scan.py</code>, which projects the cloud onto the ground plane in <code>base_footprint</code> (points 0.05 to 2 m high, within 5 m, 360 beams, beams outside the camera&#39;s view infinite). Range finders feed the costmaps&#39; range layer: <code>runtime/run_range_sensor.py</code> reports each cone&#39;s nearest return more than 0.05 m above the ground as a <code>sensor_msgs/msg/Range</code> (every simulated beam returns, a pulse glancing off the floor does not), and a reading at the maximum range clears the cone. A robot with no range sensor is warned that it navigates blind.</li><li>The simulator publishes the model pose on <code>/model/&lt;robot&gt;/pose</code> as <code>tf2_msgs/msg/TFMessage</code>. Tests and the evaluator judge a run by this, never by odometry.</li></ul><h3 id="who-owns-which-fact" tabindex="-1">Who owns which fact <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-owns-which-fact" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Who owns which fact&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Authoritative file</th><th>Also derived into</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Link and joint geometry, inertials</td><td><code>robot/robot.urdf</code></td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Wheel radius, track width, wheelbase</td><td><code>robot/controllers.yaml</code> (the controller&#39;s kinematics)</td><td><code>nav2.robot.yaml</code> velocity limits</td></tr><tr><td>Footprint</td><td><code>nav/nav2.robot.yaml</code> (both costmaps, once each)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Wheel joint names and command interfaces</td><td><code>robot/robot.urdf</code> (<code>&lt;ros2_control&gt;</code>)</td><td><code>controllers.yaml</code> names the same joints</td></tr><tr><td>Sensor topics and frames</td><td><code>robot/robot.urdf</code> (<code>&lt;gazebo&gt;</code> sensor blocks)</td><td><code>world/bridge.yaml</code>, <code>nav2.robot.yaml</code> observation sources</td></tr><tr><td>Spawn pose</td><td><code>task/mission.json</code></td><td>the launch file&#39;s spawn arguments; AMCL&#39;s initial pose in <code>nav2.robot.yaml</code></td></tr><tr><td>Obstacle positions and sizes</td><td><code>world/world.sdf</code></td><td><code>nav/map.pgm</code> rasterized from the same values; <code>task/evaluation.json</code></td></tr><tr><td>Goal poses</td><td><code>task/mission.json</code></td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Gravity, scene entities</td><td><code>world/world.sdf</code></td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Time budget</td><td><code>task/mission.json</code></td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="what-the-launch-file-substitutes-at-run-time" tabindex="-1">What the launch file substitutes at run time <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-launch-file-substitutes-at-run-time" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the launch file substitutes at run time&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>\${OUTPUT_DIR}</code> in <code>robot/robot.urdf</code> (the path of <code>controllers.yaml</code> for <code>gz_ros2_control</code>, and the mesh paths).</li><li>The path of <code>nav/map.yaml</code>, passed to the map server.</li><li>The spawn pose, read from <code>task/mission.json</code>, for the simulator and, on a robot without a laser, for the <code>map -&gt; odom</code> transform.</li></ul><p>Nothing else is computed at launch; the launch file is a fixed program over the files above. Nav2&#39;s own default behavior tree runs <code>Navigate</code>; the program&#39;s tree is not handed to <code>bt_navigator</code>.</p><h3 id="the-task" tabindex="-1">The task <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-task" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The task&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>task/behavior_tree.xml</code> is the task in BehaviorTree.CPP&#39;s XML layout with the language&#39;s node names as tags and its parameter names as attributes, so it reads like the program and opens in Groot. A goal is written by name (<code>&lt;Navigate goal=&quot;dock&quot;/&gt;</code>) and resolved through <code>task/mission.json</code>. A task whose body has several nodes runs them in order, so the body is wrapped in a <code>Sequence</code>.</p><p><code>runtime/run_task.py</code> runs the tree. It needs ROS 2 and, when the tree uses <code>Navigate</code>, <code>Spin</code>, <code>Advance</code> or <code>BackUp</code>, the <code>nav2_msgs</code> package; nothing from the compiler. Each leaf is bound as follows.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Node</th><th>Bound to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Navigate</code></td><td>Nav2 <code>navigate_to_pose</code>, goal in the <code>map</code> frame</td></tr><tr><td><code>Spin</code></td><td>Nav2 <code>spin</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Advance</code>, <code>BackUp</code></td><td>Nav2 <code>drive_on_heading</code>, <code>backup</code>; 0.15 m/s unless <code>speed_mps</code> is given</td></tr><tr><td><code>Strafe</code></td><td><code>/cmd_vel</code> with odometry feedback (Nav2 has no lateral behavior)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Wait</code></td><td>simulation time</td></tr><tr><td><code>Stop</code></td><td>zero velocity on <code>/cmd_vel</code> for half a second</td></tr><tr><td><code>GoalReached</code></td><td><code>map -&gt; base_footprint</code> within the goal tolerance of <code>nav2.base.yaml</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>DistanceTraveled</code></td><td>odometry path length since the task started</td></tr><tr><td><code>TimeExpired</code></td><td>simulation time since the task started</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The tree ticks at ten hertz. The mission&#39;s <code>timeout_s</code> ends the run with status <code>timeout</code>. <code>run/task_result.json</code> records the status (<code>success</code>, <code>failure</code>, <code>timeout</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>not_started</code>), the start and end times, one event per leaf with its parameters and outcome, the final pose the robot believes it has, and the odometry distance. It is the robot&#39;s own account.</p><h3 id="the-evaluation" tabindex="-1">The evaluation <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-evaluation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The evaluation&quot;">​</a></h3><p>While the task runs, the runtime records the robot&#39;s pose as the simulator publishes it, the joint states, and every bumper&#39;s contact reports. When the task ends it writes <code>run/evaluation.json</code> with three verdicts that rest on different evidence:</p><ul><li><code>task</code>: the status the task runtime reported.</li><li><code>physical</code>: from the recorded ground truth alone. Whether the robot ended near the last goal it navigated to (within twice the controller&#39;s goal tolerance, since the controller stops on its belief and the ground truth differs from it by the localization error), and its closest approach to any <code>Obstacle</code> over the run; touching one (within 5 mm) fails.</li><li><code>checks</code>: each check of the <code>.eval</code> file, <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code> or <code>unknown</code>, with the value it was judged on. <code>Check_vel</code>, <code>Check_acc</code>, <code>Check_jerk</code> and <code>Check_effort</code> read the joint states of the named wheel and judge every sample by magnitude; <code>Check_distance</code> and <code>Check_clearance</code> use the recorded poses and the planar shapes of <code>task/evaluation.json</code>; <code>Check_contact</code> on a <code>Bumper</code> reads the bumper&#39;s own reports (the seconds it was pressed and the scene entities that pressed it), on any other part the poses and shapes; <code>Check_time</code> uses the task&#39;s duration. The <a href="./vocabulary.html">vocabulary</a> states what each bound means.</li></ul><p>The shapes are the collision geometry the robot is simulated with, seen from above: the chassis and sensor collision boxes, each wheel as its diameter by its width, a part without a collision box as a point, and each scene entity as its box or circle. Nothing is read from odometry or from Nav2. A check on a part without a moving joint is refused at compile time; a check that has no samples to judge is <code>unknown</code>, never <code>pass</code>.</p><h3 id="approximations-this-target-makes" tabindex="-1">Approximations this target makes <a class="header-anchor" href="#approximations-this-target-makes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Approximations this target makes&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Fact in the program</th><th>What the target emits</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A mecanum wheel</td><td>A sphere collision of the wheel&#39;s radius with <code>mu = 1</code>, <code>mu2 = 0</code> and <code>fdir1</code> along the roller&#39;s free axis, expressed in <code>base_footprint</code>: <code>(1, -1, 0)</code> for the front-left and rear-right mounts, <code>(1, 1, 0)</code> for the other two</td><td>Gazebo&#39;s DART engine has no roller model; the sphere gives one contact point, and the friction direction gives the roller its free axis</td></tr><tr><td>A driven standard wheel on a two-wheel base</td><td>A sphere collision of the wheel&#39;s radius with isotropic friction <code>mu = mu2 = 3</code></td><td>One contact point keeps the controller&#39;s odometry honest; a two-wheel base turns by wheel speed difference and needs lateral grip</td></tr><tr><td>An omni wheel</td><td>A sphere collision of the wheel&#39;s radius with <code>mu = 1</code> along its rolling direction (the declared axle turned a quarter turn about up, expressed in <code>base_footprint</code>) and <code>mu2 = 0</code> along its axle</td><td>The rollers along the rim let the wheel slide along its axle; the sphere gives one contact point</td></tr><tr><td>A driven standard wheel on a skid-steer base</td><td>A sphere collision with <code>mu = 3</code> along the rolling direction and <code>mu2 = 1</code> across it, expressed in <code>base_footprint</code></td><td>A skid-steer base turns by dragging its wheels sideways; the reduced lateral friction is the slide</td></tr><tr><td>A passive wheel (caster)</td><td>A sphere collision with <code>mu = mu2 = 0.01</code></td><td>A support must slide in every direction</td></tr><tr><td>A ground contact a part declares (<code>contacts</code>)</td><td>A sphere collision of its radius on a fixed link of its own, <code>&lt;link&gt;_contact_&lt;n&gt;</code>, with <code>mu = mu2 = 0.01</code></td><td>Like a caster, a support must slide in every direction; the statics rule counts it, so the simulator has it</td></tr><tr><td>A steered wheel</td><td>A 10 g knuckle link between the chassis and the wheel, turning about the declared kingpin within its limit, with an effort limit of 50 N m and a speed limit of 3 rad/s</td><td>The library declares the kingpin&#39;s axis and limit but no knuckle body or steering actuator</td></tr><tr><td>A laser</td><td>A <code>gpu_lidar</code> with 360 beams over the full circle, 0.3 to 12 m, at 10 Hz</td><td>The library does not yet state sensor specifications</td></tr><tr><td>A spinning lidar</td><td>A <code>gpu_lidar</code> with 16 rings of 360 beams over 0.52 rad, 0.3 to 20 m, at 10 Hz</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>An IMU</td><td>An ideal <code>imu</code> sensor at 100 Hz</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>A camera</td><td>A 320 by 240 <code>camera</code>, 1.089 rad field of view, at 10 Hz</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>A depth camera</td><td>A 320 by 240 <code>depth_camera</code>, 1.089 rad field of view, 0.1 to 5 m, at 10 Hz</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>An RGBD camera</td><td>A 320 by 240 <code>rgbd_camera</code>, 1.089 rad field of view, 0.1 to 5 m, at 10 Hz</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>An ultrasonic range finder</td><td>A <code>gpu_lidar</code> of 9 by 9 beams filling a 0.52 rad cone, 0.02 to 4 m, at 20 Hz, whose nearest return is the reading</td><td>The simulator has no range finder</td></tr><tr><td>An ultrasonic range finder&#39;s reading</td><td>Made at run time from the cone&#39;s returns more than 0.05 m above the ground</td><td>A pulse glancing off the floor does not echo; every simulated beam returns</td></tr><tr><td>A bumper</td><td>An ideal <code>contact</code> sensor over its collision box, read at 50 Hz; the bumper keeps its own link so that the sensor can name the collision</td><td>The library does not state the sensor&#39;s specification</td></tr><tr><td>A spinning lidar as the only planar range sensor</td><td>Its middle ring as the scan the costmaps and the localizer read</td><td>Nav2 and AMCL read laser scans</td></tr><tr><td>A depth or RGBD camera as the only range sensor</td><td>Its cloud projected onto a planar scan for the costmaps and the localizer</td><td>As above</td></tr><tr><td>A part without a collision box</td><td>Its visual mesh as collision geometry</td><td>Nothing else is known</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The friction values are the ones the previous compiler drove its experiments with. Each approximation is listed in <code>compile.json</code> for the component that triggered it.</p><h3 id="not-emitted" tabindex="-1">Not emitted <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-emitted" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not emitted&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A program that uses any of these stops with a <code>target.unsupported</code> diagnostic naming the construct, and nothing is written: an omni base whose rolling directions do not span the plane, a steering base whose chassis declares no kingpin, a scene entity with <code>ref</code>, a scene entity declared movable (<code>is_static = false</code>: an entity states no mass to move it with), a <code>Scenario</code> whose <code>ref</code> is not <code>empty</code>, a joint check on a part without a moving joint, and a component named <code>base_link</code> or <code>base_footprint</code>.</p><h2 id="the-ros-mujoco-nav2-target" tabindex="-1">The <code>ros-mujoco-nav2</code> target <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-ros-mujoco-nav2-target" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The \`ros-mujoco-nav2\` target&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The second target keeps everything above that is ROS: the robot description (<code>robot/robot.urdf</code>, with a <code>&lt;mujoco&gt;</code> compiler block MuJoCo&#39;s URDF reader takes and none of Gazebo&#39;s blocks), the controllers, the navigation parameters, the map, the task files and the runtime, and swaps the simulator. Its <code>world/</code> holds <code>mujoco.json</code> instead of an SDF and a bridge: the ground, the scene entities, the actuators on the wheel and kingpin joints, the wheels&#39; contact friction, the sensors with the topics they publish on, and the spawn pose. <code>runtime/run_mujoco_sim.py</code> loads the description and that scene, steps the physics paced to the wall clock, publishes <code>/clock</code>, and speaks to ros2_control through its topic-based hardware (<code>/mujoco/joint_states</code>, <code>/mujoco/joint_commands</code>). It publishes the robot&#39;s pose on <code>/model/&lt;robot&gt;/pose</code> and every sensor on the same topics as the Gazebo target, computed from the physics state: lasers and range finders cast rays (the robot never sees itself, and the floor never returns), cameras render offscreen (OSMesa by default, <code>MUJOCO_GL=egl</code> with a GPU), an IMU reads the body&#39;s motion, a bumper reads the contacts. The launch takes the same arguments; <code>headless</code> has no effect.</p><p>MuJoCo&#39;s contact friction is isotropic, so it cannot yet stand in for a mecanum or omni wheel, which rolls freely along its axle: a base on such wheels is refused with <code>target.unsupported</code> and compiles for <code>ros-gz-nav2</code> instead. A part that declares no collision box is refused too: the Gazebo target collides such a part through its visual mesh, which the library ships as Collada, and MuJoCo reads meshes only as STL, OBJ or MSH. The world&#39;s gravity and ground plane are the program&#39;s, as in Gazebo, and so is the refusals of a base environment other than <code>&quot;empty&quot;</code> and of an entity declared movable. Its own stand-ins, declared in <code>compile.json</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Construct</th><th>Emitted</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A wheel</td><td>A sphere contact of the wheel&#39;s radius with isotropic friction 1, 0.01 for a wheel that does not roll</td><td>MuJoCo&#39;s contact friction is isotropic; a rolling wheel grips, a caster slides</td></tr><tr><td>A ground contact a part declares</td><td>A sphere contact of its radius on the Gazebo target&#39;s fixed link, with isotropic friction 0.01</td><td>Like a caster, a support slides in every direction</td></tr><tr><td>The drive</td><td>A velocity actuator of gain 0.5 N m s/rad on each driven wheel, a position actuator of gains 50 N m/rad and 2 N m s/rad on each kingpin</td><td>The library does not state the drives&#39; response</td></tr><tr><td>A range finder</td><td>Its reading from the cone&#39;s nearest return, the floor never returning</td><td>A pulse glancing off the floor does not echo; the rays skip it</td></tr><tr><td>A skid-steer base</td><td>A base that turns in place at about a third of the commanded rate while its wheel odometry reports the commanded turn</td><td>MuJoCo&#39;s isotropic friction resists a wheel dragged across its rolling direction as much as it grips along it</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The test image is <code>robotdsl/ros-mujoco-nav2:jazzy</code>, built from <code>docker/ros-mujoco-nav2/jazzy/Dockerfile</code>.</p>`,41)])])}const u=t(n,[["render",d]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
